WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:02.670
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:06.060
I help some texture compression
gurus solve their bucket

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.260
creation problems.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.852
Will we zip up
their performance?

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:09.810
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.900
Encode A La Mode came to
us with a small problem.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.820
Their cloud-based texture
compression service

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.755
was handling 23,000
encodings a day.

00:00:20.755 --> 00:00:22.380
And some of their
clients were starting

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.960
to notice that the time it
took to export their data

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.115
was starting to get
significantly longer.

00:00:27.115 --> 00:00:28.740
Their system works
something like this.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.156
For each build,
their script would

00:00:30.156 --> 00:00:32.414
create a new bucket
in cloud storage,

00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:34.080
upload all the textures
from the client,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.180
and then kick off work
to a load balancer, where

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.310
a bunch of Compute
Engine instances

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.010
would start converting textures.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.300
Their setup required that
for each batch of textures

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.370
to be uploaded, a
unique bucket was

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.140
created so that they
can more easily track

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.037
the progress of encoding state.

00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:49.620
Although their setup
was pretty sound,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.411
they couldn't ignore
their customers' notes

00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:54.600
about performance, so it was
time to dig in and figure out

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.874
the problem.

00:00:55.874 --> 00:00:58.290
Our first step was to check
the load balancing and scaling

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.070
operation.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.260
If everything was set
up right, new instances

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.100
should be spun up properly.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.640
So to check that out, we
looked at the CPU utilization

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.840
for the instance group.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.116
The values were low,
about in the 12% range,

00:01:09.116 --> 00:01:11.490
and there was only about three
to four instances running.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.860
So the problem was
not that the instance

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.210
were getting overloaded.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.370
Now, with CPU rates
that low, the next thing

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.860
we had to check to make
sure was that machines

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.140
weren't getting stalled,
crashing, or hitting

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.550
a weird error condition.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.950
But according to the logs,
they were receiving data

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:26.620
right on time.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.770
And, in fact, most of the
instances in the group

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.110
were sitting around
just waiting for work.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.450
With the instance group
removed as a potential source

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.210
of the problem, we set
our gaze to the GCS bucket

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.930
and how the team was using it.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.590
And, lo and behold, ran
right into the issue.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.510
It turns out that there
is a per-project rate

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.760
limit to bucket creation and
deletion of approximately

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.240
one operation every two seconds,
according to the documentation.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.980
For them, this meant
that if 30 artists

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.660
across multiple
companies all tried

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.850
to export at the same
time, then random folks

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.760
would start getting
exception 429 to be thrown,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.030
signaling that the rate was
exceeded for bucket creation.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.100
Where this turned into
a performance problem

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.860
was that the Python API
their tool was using

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.620
had to implement an exponential
back-off and retry, which

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.940
is nice and common practice.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.790
But since the queue
was getting backed up,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.830
folks were ending up
waiting a longer time

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.630
for a simple export.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.690
Turns out they didn't need that
much dynamic bucket creation.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.480
Instead they created one
bucket for each artist

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.450
as they registered
with the system.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.130
This removed the entire
bucket creation bottleneck,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.440
making things a lot
faster and made it easier

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.440
to handle tracking down what
assets were causing problems

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.121
in the case of an error.

00:02:33.121 --> 00:02:35.370
To learn more about the perf
details of these buckets,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.620
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.744
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:39.744 --> 00:02:40.889
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:42.430
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.650
every millisecond counts.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.000
